  $j$[[0.3, "Welcome to the Point of View mini-stories for The Role of Media 1."], [8.2, "Let's get started."], [9.3, "Same story, but different time frames."], [12.5, "Our first one, as always, we'll start with the past."], [15.7, "20 years ago."], [17.8, "20 years ago, there was an English teacher named AJ."], [23.2, "One day, he went to English class, and one student named Aki complained."], [29.3, "He said, AJ, your class sucks."], [33.4, "It's boring."], [35.5, "AJ was surprised."], [36.8, "He asked the class, is this the prevailing attitude?"], [40.7, "Is this the prevailing opinion?"], [42.8, "Do you all agree?"], [44.7, "They all shouted together, yes, you suck."], [49.5, "Then they attacked AJ."], [51.1, "They hit him and beat him."], [53.1, "They threw him out of the room and shouted, you are barred from this class."], [57.8, "Never come back."], [61.0, "A.J. was sad, but he decided to get revenge."], [65.2, "He decided to fight again, so he joined a ninja school to learn how to fight."], [71.0, "He said to the ninja teacher."], [73.5, "I want to learn to kill someone with a knife and a sword."], [78.1, "The teacher said,"], [79.8, "AJ, your conceptions of the means of ninja fighting are old and wrong."], [86.2, "Contemporary American ninjas use guns."], [88.7, "Then he began to teach AJ how to fight and how to use guns like a ninja."], [97.1, "He yelled, don't stand rigidly, make your body soft and flexible."], [102.6, "Every day AJ practiced."], [104.6, "Finally, he was ready."], [106.6, "He was strong and dangerous."], [109.0, "The teacher said, AJ, counterpose your old self to your new self."], [115.0, "Then you were weak, now you are strong."], [119.0, "AJ said, yes, master, I am strong."], [122.4, "I am now ready to destroy my evil English class."], [127.4, "As AJ walked out the door, the master yelled, keep to your ninja methods, and destroy them all."], [136.3, "All right, very good, let's do it again."], [140.9, "This time we're going to start with the phrase “since last month”."], [147.9, "You ready? Since last month, let's do it."], [151.7, "All right, there's a teacher named AJ."], [155.1, "Since last month he has gone to his English class and every day Aki has complained."], [164.9, "Aki has said, AJ, your class sucks, it's boring."], [171.5, "AJ has always been sad."], [174.9, "He's always been surprised."], [177.7, "Well finally, one day, he asked the class."], [182.9, "Is this the prevailing opinion?"], [184.9, "Do you all agree?"], [186.8, "Well, they all agreed."], [188.0, "They all shouted together, Yes, you suck!"], [191.4, "Then they attacked AJ."], [192.8, "They threw him out of the room and shouted, You are barred from this class."], [197.1, "Never come back."], [200.7, "AJ was sad, but he decided to get revenge."], [203.4, "So he joined a ninja school to learn how to fight."], [207.0, "He said to the ninja teacher."], [209.1, "I want to learn to kill someone with a knife and a sword."], [213.4, "The teacher said, Your conceptions of the means of ninja fighting are old and wrong."], [219.9, "Contemporary American ninjas use guns."], [223.1, "He then began to teach AJ how to fight and how to use guns like a ninja."], [230.0, "Every day, he would yell, don't stand rigidly."], [234.3, "Make your body soft and flexible."], [237.9, "Every day, AJ practiced."], [241.4, "Finally, he was ready."], [242.6, "He was strong and dangerous."], [244.6, "The teacher said, counterpose your old self to your new self."], [249.7, "Before you were weak, Look, now you are strong!"], [254.9, "AJ said, yes master, I am strong, I am now ready to destroy my evil English class."], [262.8, "As AJ walked out the door, the master yelled."], [266.2, "Keep to your ninja training."], [267.9, "Keep to your ninja methods."], [269.9, "And destroy them all."], [273.2, "Alright, now it's time for our third version, going to the future, let's imagine this is movie idea."], [280.2, "Or maybe a dream."], [282.0, "Okay?"], [282.3, "So we're thinking about the future."], [284.1, "I have a dream and I'm thinking this is going to happen."], [286.9, "This is going to happen to me."], [289.1, "So one day, AJ will go to English class."], [293.9, "One of the students named Aki will complain."], [296.8, "He'll say, AJ, your class sucks."], [300.3, "It's boring."], [301.8, "AJ will be surprised."], [303.6, "He'll ask the class."], [305.1, "Is this the prevailing opinion?"], [307.1, "Do you all agree?"], [309.2, "They'll all shout together."], [311.4, "Yes, you suck!"], [313.9, "Then they'll attack AJ."], [315.7, "They'll throw him out of the room and they'll shout, you are barred from this class."], [320.2, "Never come back."], [322.3, "AJ is gonna be sad."], [324.4, "He'll decide to get revenge though."], [327.2, "So he'll join a ninja school to learn how to fight."], [330.8, "He'll say to the ninja teacher."], [332.7, "I want to learn to kill someone with a knife and a sword."], [337.1, "But the teacher's going to say, look."], [340.2, "Your conceptions of the means of ninja fighting are old and wrong."], [346.0, "Contemporary American ninjas use guns."], [349.5, "He'll then begin to teach AJ how to fight and how to use guns like a ninja."], [354.7, "He'll yell, don't stand rigidly, Make your body soft and flexible."], [359.7, "Every day, AJ will practice."], [362.6, "Finally, he'll be ready."], [365.3, "He'll be strong and dangerous."], [367.8, "The teacher's gonna say, Counterpose your old self to your new self."], [373.9, "Then you are weak, now you are strong."], [377.1, "AJ will say, Yes, master, I am strong."], [381.1, "I am now ready to destroy my evil English class."], [385.4, "Now, as AJ walks out the door, the master will say."], [390.4, "Keep to your ninja methods and destroy them all!"], [395.0, "Okay, that is all for our POV point of view stories."], [399.8, "Please pause now, go back, listen to version number one, the past tense, 20 years ago."], [407.3, "Listen to it and then stop and try to tell all of the story yourself using the past tense."], [412.5, "Then listen to the second one."], [414.5, "The second one is a mix."], [415.8, "It usually is a mix."], [416.8, "You've probably noticed, right?"], [418.5, "There'll be a little bit of present perfect, actually, you know, have gone, have been."], [424.1, "And then there'll also be a good amount of the past."], [427.2, "So you want to begin that one with since last month, right?"], [431.4, "AJ has gone to his English class since last month."], [434.0, "The student has complained, et cetera."], [435.8, "Listen to it and then try to tell it yourself."], [438.8, "And finally, listen to the third version about the future."], [441.8, "Listen to it, stop, try to tell it yourself in the future."], [446.4, "Okay, listen for those “'ll” sounds, right?"], [448.7, "AJ'll."], [449.5, "A-J-OLL."], [450.7, "We don't usually say “AJ will”."], [453.6, "That's written English, but in speaking we just add that “'ll” sound."], [457.8, "AJ'll go to the store."], [459.8, "A-J-OLL go to the store."], [460.9, "AJ will go to the store."], [462.4, "We just say AJ'll."], [464.2, "So listen for that."], [465.5, "Also listen for “gonna”."], [466.9, "“Gonna” means “going to”."], [468.5, "And that's also for the future, spoken future."], [471.7, "Alright, good luck."], [473.3, "Listen to these many times, if necessary, until they feel natural and easy."], [478.4, "And then, move on, listen to the article, and listen to the article many times."], [484.0, "See you next time."], [484.8, "Bye-bye."]]$j$::jsonb,
  $j$[[0.6, "Hello, and welcome to the Role of Media 1 lesson."], [6.7, "This is the commentary."], [8.6, "I'd like to talk a little bit about Noam Chomsky in this commentary."], [12.8, "He's the writer of this article."], [14.9, "There will be more."], [15.9, "This is from a book of his called Media Control by Noam Chomsky."], [21.0, "And we'll talk more about the actual issues in future commentaries."], [26.5, "But this time I just want to tell you more about Noam Chomsky, the man."], [30.0, "Who is he?"], [31.6, "He's a very, very interesting guy."], [34.3, "Noam Chomsky kind of has two careers."], [37.3, "He's famous for two different things."], [40.0, "Number one, he's a famous linguist."], [42.8, "In fact, he's probably the most famous linguist in the world."], [47.7, "Now, when I say linguist, I don't mean he speaks a lot of languages."], [51.7, "What I mean is he studies languages."], [54.1, "How do languages work?"], [57.1, "What is grammar exactly?"], [59.4, "And how does it work?"], [60.6, "Not just in English, but in all languages."], [63.8, "And he has a lot of very interesting theories that are very famous."], [69.0, "One of them is called the Theory of Universal Grammar."], [72.5, "And that basically says that while every language has different grammar."], [77.5, "Some different grammar, that underneath there is some basic grammar, some basic language principles which are the same for all languages."], [88.2, "And that's why we can learn other languages because they're different but they're not totally different, right?"], [95.0, "So a Japanese person can learn English."], [97.4, "Yes, they're kind of different but really, if you go deeper, there are things that are very similar and that's why we can, people can learn other languages."], [106.8, "But anyway, there's a lot more complicated theories, but the point is Noam Chomsky is a famous linguist."], [112.8, "That's what he first was famous for."], [116.0, "And he still is a very famous linguist, probably the most famous linguist."], [120.5, "When I got my master's degree in teaching English as a foreign language, I studied Noam Chomsky."], [127.2, "Everybody does when they take a linguistics course."], [130.1, "Okay, now he's a professor at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, MIT."], [137.4, "MIT is probably the number one technology university in America."], [144.3, "I don't know, number one or number two."], [146.6, "The top two are Caltech and MIT."], [150.4, "And he's a professor at MIT, so this is like the Harvard of technical schools, so science type of school."], [159.8, "So he's been a professor there a long time."], [163.0, "Now his second field, number field number one is linguistics, but he's also famous for politics, for political science."], [170.9, "In fact, most people know him because of this."], [175.0, "He's famous all around the world for his politics and his political research."], [181.4, "And he's a very interesting guy."], [184.3, "Noam Chomsky has done a lot of research about modern democracies, especially the United States."], [190.9, "And what he did."], [192.4, "And continues to do."], [194.4, "is he gets documents,"], [196.9, "official government documents, from past governments in the United States."], [202.0, "So he'll get official documents, for example."], [204.6, "From President Kennedy, you know, in the 60s, President Johnson from the 60s, and then going back to the 50s, and the 40s, and the 30s, and the 20s."], [214.0, "It's possible to get these documents now."], [217.4, "At that time in the past, they were secret."], [221.0, "No one could see them, but now we have some laws in America that allow us to get those documents."], [228.3, "So he's gotten these documents and he's researched, you know, what does the government really do?"], [234.4, "What does the American government really think, the people at the top?"], [238.4, "What are they really trying to do?"], [240.1, "And he's found out that, in fact."], [242.7, "The number one thing they do is keep control for large corporations and for people who are very, very rich."], [251.6, "Now, that's no surprise to me, but a lot of people think, oh my God, that's not possible."], [256.3, "That's not true."], [258.0, "America is only about freedom and democracy."], [261.8, "We're always right."], [263.0, "We always do the good thing."], [265.1, "But he's found, in fact, that is not the main concern of the government of the United States."], [270.9, "But he does admit, he does say, that in fact, America has a very free society, it's true."], [276.4, "But because we have a free society."], [278.7, "The government has to find another way to control the people."], [283.0, "Right?"], [284.4, "Mostly, the government doesn't grab people and throw them in jail, or shoot them and kill them, because they disagree with the government."], [291.2, "You know, this happens in some countries, China, for example, is maybe the perfect example."], [297.0, "So in China, if you yell something against the government on the street, the police grab you and take you to prison and torture you and maybe kill you."], [304.9, "And usually in America, that does not happen."], [308.5, "Not yet."], [309.5, "George Bush is working on it."], [311.6, "But anyway, still mostly free."], [315.3, "And so he says that since the democratic governments, especially the United States, can't control with this kind of force usually."], [324.6, "Instead they control people with propaganda."], [327.2, "They use basically mind control."], [329.6, "They control with the media."], [331.2, "They control people's thoughts and ideas to make them do what they want them to do."], [336.2, "And he has studied these government documents and done a lot of his own research about how they do that."], [343.6, "How do they use the media?"], [345.2, "How do they use propaganda to control the public, to control what the public thinks, and what the public does, and what the public will support?"], [354.5, "Very interesting, and, you know, a little scary sometimes."], [357.7, "But he's not a crazy guy; he's a very intelligent guy, and all of this is based on research."], [364.4, "He's not some crazy guy who just says, oh my God, the government's bad."], [369.4, "He's very intelligent, very careful about what he does."], [372.6, "And this article, this Role of Media 1, comes from a book of his called Media Control."], [378.7, "And i will be doing more parts from this book very interesting book."], [384.9, "And in fact you know my."], [387.0, "You may or may not know but uh..."], [389.5, "My first degree uh..."], [391.8, "Is in journalism i studied journalism as an undergraduate i have a bachelor's degree in journalism and uh..."], [398.0, "That's when i first learned about noam chompski uh..."], [400.7, "Because i was studying his ideas about media."], [405.9, "And I saw him speak, in fact, at that time."], [408.9, "He came to my university, the University of Georgia."], [411.8, "He came to my university, in fact, during the first Gulf War."], [416.3, "George Bush's father, right, George Bush number one."], [420.6, "And he came and he spoke about the war."], [423.0, "The war was getting ready to start."], [425.4, "You know, we, everybody knew it was going to happen, and he came, he was going around speaking to universities."], [430.3, "And he talked about how the American government and the media were using all this propaganda."], [438.2, "To make Americans support the war."], [440.9, "To make them afraid of Saddam Hussein."], [443.4, "To make them really, yes, let's go, let's attack, yes, yes, yes."], [446.8, "And he talked about in very clear detail how this was happening and that it was not an accident that the government."], [453.5, "And not only government, but big business as well."], [456.5, "The media companies were doing this, and it was a very effective campaign."], [461.6, "And he was right, of course, and of course they've done the same thing again with this second Iraq war."], [469.4, "But anyway, I saw him that first time speaking, and I was impressed."], [474.0, "I was like, wow, this guy is very intelligent and very interesting."], [478.3, "And since then, I've read several of his books, actually."], [481.9, "They're a little difficult; he's an academic, he's a, you know, Ph.D., so some of his books are quite difficult."], [489.0, "This one that I chose, this media control book that I'll be using for lessons, is not so difficult."], [496.7, "There might be parts that are a little tough."], [499.4, "Especially if your level is a little lower, but for the advanced students, the advanced members of Effortless English, you should be able to understand it."], [507.6, "Also, most of the chapters are going to be quite short."], [511.2, "I'll edit them, them, I'll make them a little shorter and easier to understand."], [515.9, "So I think the ideas are very interesting and very important."], [519.9, "So I want to present this to you all and in general I want to use interesting, thought-provoking material for you to learn English."], [532.2, "Maybe the worst part about textbooks is that they're irrelevant."], [537.1, "Irrelevant means they have no use at all."], [540.9, "You read any English textbook."], [542.9, "Any English textbook, and you have these little fake articles and these little fake conversations."], [547.3, "Well, who cares?"], [548.0, "They're not real people."], [549.3, "They're not real ideas."], [551.2, "It's all fake."], [552.6, "So you have no reason to be curious."], [555.1, "You have no reason to be interested in the language."], [558.6, "It's not important."], [559.4, "It's totally artificial."], [561.2, "So all you do is you focus on the language itself."], [564.2, "You spend all your time worrying about verbs and adverbs and phrasal verbs and, you know, different parts."], [573.4, "Tenses and parts of speech and et cetera, et cetera, memorizing the vocabulary."], [579.2, "Doing this fill in the blank exercise, you know, is it A or B or C?"], [583.5, "And you get all focused and after a while, that's what English is for a lot of students."], [590.4, "English is stupid articles that are meaningless."], [594.7, "Stupid conversations."], [596.0, "which are not real and have no meaning,"], [598.3, "between people who are not real."], [600.5, "And then meaningless stupid activities where you're filling in a blank or choosing A, B, C, or D."], [609.5, "And that's English."], [612.1, "Well, it's not English."], [613.3, "That is not English."], [614.4, "English is what is used every day in the United States and England and the world actually now to communicate real ideas that are important and now they may be jokes, they may be funny ideas, they may be stories."], [629.4, "They may be very deep philosophical ideas."], [634.4, "They may be just basic information you need for your life, you know, how much is this."], [639.2, "When does the train come, but it's real information that matters, that has some meaning to you."], [645.8, "That's what English is."], [647.2, "That's what any language is."], [648.7, "That's a real language."], [650.2, "And textbooks destroy that."], [652.3, "That's why I hate them."], [653.7, "I hate them because they kill the language."], [659.0, "And that's why so many students, you know, they tell me, oh, I hate English."], [663.7, "It's so boring."], [665.5, "Oh, why do I need it?"], [667.0, "I don't care."], [668.2, "Or maybe they really do need it."], [669.7, "They need it for something but they hate it."], [672.2, "Oh, English is horrible."], [674.1, "It's so boring."], [674.9, "Well, English is not boring."], [676.6, "The textbooks are boring."], [678.0, "The classes are boring."], [679.3, "The teachers are probably boring."], [682.0, "Certainly the tests are boring and the activities are boring, but English itself is not boring."], [687.3, "It's only because you're reading the wrong things and listening to the wrong things."], [691.5, "There are some really amazing ideas out there."], [694.9, "There are some amazing books in English."], [697.2, "There are some amazing podcasts in English."], [700.5, "There are amazing movies in English."], [701.8, "There are amazing people who have great ideas who speak English."], [706.6, "I think Noam Chomsky is one of them."], [709.6, "And that's the purpose of the language, to find out this great interesting person, wow, what are his ideas?"], [716.6, "Now that's, that's language, that's real English."], [720.0, "And that's what I want to focus on."], [723.3, "Now it might, it's not always going to be serious."], [725.5, "Sometimes language is funny."], [726.9, "You know, the Dear Abby letters, for example, they're kind of funny, sometimes they're certainly not so serious."], [732.3, "But you know that it's real life."], [733.8, "Those are real people writing those letters."], [736.0, "Those problems are real."], [737.8, "Somewhere in America, someone is having that problem."], [740.9, "So it's still, it's real."], [742.7, "There's a real emotion there."], [744.3, "There are real ideas."], [745.8, "These are real lives."], [747.4, "And that's how you make English real."], [750.6, "You've got to connect with real thoughts, real emotions, real people, real lives."], [756.4, "That's what will help you the most."], [759.1, "More important than how you study, more important than anything else."], [763.5, "Is connecting with the real language and the emotion and the thoughts and the people who speak it."], [770.4, "Forget everything else."], [771.6, "If you can do that, you will enjoy the language and you'll want to learn it and everything else will happen automatically."]]$j$::jsonb,
  $j$[["role", "/rəʊl/", "vai trò"], ["media", "/ˈmiːdiə/", "truyền thông"], ["contemporary", "/kənˈtemprəri/", "đương đại"], ["politics", "/ˈpɒlətɪks/", "chính trị"], ["force someone to", "/fɔːs ˈsʌmwʌn tuː/", "buộc ai phải"], ["in particular", "/ɪn pəˈtɪkjələ/", "đặc biệt là"], ["democracy", "/dɪˈmɒkrəsi/", "nền dân chủ"], ["democratic", "/ˌdeməˈkrætɪk/", "dân chủ"], ["counterpose", "/ˈkaʊntəpəʊz/", "đặt đối lập"], ["conception", "/kənˈsepʃn/", "quan niệm"], ["the public", "/ðə ˈpʌblɪk/", "công chúng"], ["means", "/miːnz/", "phương tiện"], ["participate in", "/pɑːˈtɪsɪpeɪt ɪn/", "tham gia vào"], ["meaningful", "/ˈmiːnɪŋfl/", "có ý nghĩa"], ["management", "/ˈmænɪdʒmənt/", "sự quản lý"], ["affairs", "/əˈfeəz/", "công việc"], ["look up", "/lʊk ʌp/", "tra cứu"], ["definition", "/ˌdefɪˈnɪʃn/", "định nghĩa"], ["alternative", "/ɔːlˈtɜːnətɪv/", "khác, thay thế"], ["bar from", "/bɑː frɒm/", "ngăn cấm"], ["rigidly", "/ˈrɪdʒɪdli/", "cứng nhắc"], ["odd", "/ɒd/", "kỳ quặc"], ["prevailing", "/prɪˈveɪlɪŋ/", "thịnh hành"], ["in operation", "/ɪn ˌɒpəˈreɪʃn/", "đang vận hành"], ["in theory", "/ɪn ˈθɪəri/", "về lý thuyết"], ["go back to", "/ɡəʊ bæk tuː/", "bắt nguồn từ"], ["revolution", "/ˌrevəˈluːʃn/", "cuộc cách mạng"], ["largely", "/ˈlɑːdʒli/", "phần lớn"], ["notion", "/ˈnəʊʃn/", "khái niệm"], ["disinformation", "/ˌdɪsɪnfəˈmeɪʃn/", "thông tin sai lệch"], ["context", "/ˈkɒntekst/", "bối cảnh"]]$j$::jsonb,
  $j$[["the role of the media in contemporary politics", "vai trò của truyền thông trong chính trị đương đại"], ["forces us to ask", "buộc chúng ta phải hỏi"], ["what kind of a society we want to live in", "chúng ta muốn sống trong xã hội như thế nào"], ["in particular", "đặc biệt là"], ["Let me begin by", "Hãy để tôi bắt đầu bằng"], ["two different conceptions of democracy", "hai quan niệm khác nhau về dân chủ"], ["participate in some meaningful way", "tham gia một cách có ý nghĩa"], ["the management of their own affairs", "việc quản lý công việc của chính họ"], ["open and free", "cởi mở và tự do"], ["If you look up democracy in the dictionary", "nếu bạn tra từ dân chủ trong từ điển"], ["something like that", "đại loại như vậy"], ["barred from managing their own affairs", "bị ngăn không được tự quản lý công việc"], ["narrowly and rigidly controlled", "bị kiểm soát chặt chẽ, cứng nhắc"], ["That may sound like", "Nghe có vẻ như"], ["the prevailing conception", "quan niệm đang thịnh hành"], ["not just in operation, but even in theory", "không chỉ trong thực tiễn mà cả lý thuyết"], ["goes back to", "bắt nguồn từ"], ["say a few words about", "nói đôi lời về"]]$j$::jsonb,
  $j$[["S + force(s) + someone + to + V", "The rain forced us to stay home.", "Cơn mưa buộc chúng tôi ở nhà.", "forces|forced|force"], ["what kind of + noun + S + V", "I don't know what kind of job I want.", "Tôi không biết mình muốn công việc kiểu gì.", "what kind of"], ["Let me begin by + V-ing", "Let me begin by thanking you all.", "Cho phép tôi bắt đầu bằng việc cảm ơn tất cả các bạn.", "let me begin"], ["a + noun + in which + clause", "This is a city in which people feel safe.", "Đây là thành phố mà người dân cảm thấy an toàn.", "in which"], ["S + have the means to + V", "They don't have the means to buy a house.", "Họ không có điều kiện mua nhà.", "the means to"], ["If you look up + word + in the dictionary, ...", "If you look up this word in the dictionary, you'll see two meanings.", "Nếu tra từ này trong từ điển, bạn sẽ thấy hai nghĩa.", "look up"], ["S + be + barred from + V-ing", "He was barred from entering the club.", "Anh ta bị cấm vào câu lạc bộ.", "barred from"], ["That may sound + adj/like + noun, but ...", "That may sound strange, but it's true.", "Nghe có vẻ lạ, nhưng đó là sự thật.", "may sound"], ["It's important to understand that + clause", "It's important to understand that change takes time.", "Điều quan trọng là phải hiểu rằng thay đổi cần thời gian.", "important to understand"], ["not just A, but even B", "She speaks not just English, but even Japanese.", "Cô ấy nói không chỉ tiếng Anh mà cả tiếng Nhật.", "not just"], ["S + go(es) back to + time/noun", "This tradition goes back to the 15th century.", "Truyền thống này bắt nguồn từ thế kỷ 15.", "goes back to|go back to"], ["I'm just going to + V", "I'm just going to say a few words.", "Tôi chỉ nói đôi lời thôi.", "just going to"]]$j$::jsonb
